.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1230 AM PDT Wed Oct 21 2026
(Today and tonight)

The center of the 500 mb cut-low is inching closer to the coast as
the sub-tropical jet stream supports the left portion of the system,
bringing enhanced momentum and moisture flux. This is where the most
instability and lift can be found. As of 11:30 PM PDT, KMUX radar is
picking up on a few cells offshore exhibiting a ~10-25% chance of
lightning with a few lightning strikes observed along the occluded
front (~250-300 miles SW from Monterey). The current offshore
activity is foreshadowing what is to come early Wednesday morning.

The first few rain bands are expected to make their way over the
Central Coast just before sunrise. Going into the afternoon,
precipitation gets wrapped around the system`s center and eventually
spreads through the rest of the Bay Area. Precipitable water is
expected to rise above the 90th percentile for this time of year,
which prompted WPC to include the southern Central Coast in a
marginal risk of excessive rainfall for Wednesday.

To further assess the potential for severe thunderstorms, three high-
resolution model soundings were analyzed: the 3km NAM, 10km RDPS,
and the RRFS. Keep in mind that the NAM tends to be the most
pessimistic model out of the three in terms of moisture. Upon
evaluation of the near-storm environment for each, there are a few
inconsistencies across the models in terms of the severe
thunderstorm threat and whether the environment is conducive to
tornado spin-ups.

Before getting into what *is* consistent across the models, let`s
run through a quick overview of a couple notable stability indices:
the Total-Totals and K index. The Total-Totals index measures
atmospheric instability by taking the thermal difference between 850
to 500 mb to identify the potential for severe thunderstorms or
tornadoes. The K index is used to assess atmospheric buoyancy by
measuring the low to mid-level moisture as well as the 850-500 mb
thermal difference. The greater the K index value, the greater the
potential is for widespread thunderstorms (K > 20 = scattered
thunderstorms, K > 35 = numerous thunderstorms). Similarly, higher
Total-Totals (TT) index values indicate an enhanced potential for
severe thunderstorms and/or tornadoes (TT > 48 = severe
thunderstorms, TT > 50 = tornadoes).

Why am I talking about stability indices? Well, according to these
indices and other environmental parameters, the three high-res model
soundings that were analyzed all indicate a heightened risk for at
least scattered, severe thunderstorms along the Big Sur coastline.
The NAM estimates TT ~ 56 and a K index ~ 36, RDPS estimates TT ~ 54
and a K index ~ 30, and RRFS estimates TT ~ 50 and K index ~ 31.

Another consistency found across the model soundings is the amount
of low-level wind shear. For our area, a small amount of shear goes
a long way and can support low-topped supercells and/or brief spin-
ups of waterspouts/tornadoes. From Wednesday afternoon through early
Thursday morning, the models agree on low-level 0-1 km shear in
excess of 10-15 kt (12-17 mph). This time frame coincides with when
the greatest severe weather threat may occur. The NAM wants to fully
lock in the tornado threat, though the RDPS and RRFS are split
50/50. The SPC issued a 2-4% tornado probability for the Big Sur
coastline, thereby indicating increased potential for some low-
topped supercells and near-coastal, potentially landfalling,
waterspouts.

If severe weather does develop, here are some safety reminders:

* Have multiple ways to receive emergency alerts (i.e. NOAA weather
  radio, follow NWS Bay Area on social media, and/or ensuring your
  phone`s emergency alert system is on)
* Move to an interior room away from windows, if a waterspout makes
  landfall
* Have an emergency action plan ready if you live downslope of
  wildfire burn scars
* Avoid flooded roads. Turn around, don`t drown!

By late Thursday morning, some lingering showers persist through the
region with the potential for general thunderstorms and no
waterspout/tornado threat across the Central Coast. Max temperatures
remain in the mid 50s to low 70s as onshore winds gradually increase
through the evening.

